Subject: Revised Findings Summary – Security Deposit & Post-Move-Out Charges

Dear Mr. Eastman:

I hope you are doing well. I am writing to follow up with a summary of our findings after reviewing the additional documents you provided regarding the security deposit and post-move-out charges assessed by Kairos Living for the property at 226 Shadow Street NW, Port Charlotte, Florida 33952.

As discussed, the primary concern is whether Kairos Living properly complied with Florida Statute §83.49 in issuing its May 30, 2025 notice of intent to impose a claim on your deposit, and whether the landlord retains any legal right to offset damages against the deposit.

Key Findings
1. Vacate Date & Lease Term
• Your written lease term ran through April 30, 2025.
• On February 7, 2025, you notified Kairos of your intent to vacate effective April 15, 2025.
• On February 10, 2025, Rebecca from Kairos confirmed receipt of your notice.
• Your utility records show service at your new residence began April 15, 2025, with the prior residence scheduled to end April 30.
• On April 15, 2025, Kairos emailed requesting your forwarding address for mailing any potential refund.
• This documentation establishes April 15, 2025 as the date you vacated and surrendered possession, notwithstanding that the lease term ran through April 30.

✅ Conclusion: For purposes of Florida Statute §83.49, the controlling date is the actual move-out and surrender of possession (April 15, 2025), not the lease expiration date (April 30, 2025).

2. Security Deposit Dispute
• Kairos issued its notice of intent to impose a claim on May 30, 2025.
• Florida Statute §83.49(3)(a) requires the landlord to provide notice within 30 days “after the tenant vacates the premises.”
• Because you vacated on April 15, the May 30 notice was served 45 days later—15 days beyond the statutory deadline.
• As a result, Kairos forfeited its right to impose any claim against your deposit, and you are entitled to a refund of the entire $2,225 deposit.
• A later move-out accounting letter dated June 18, 2025 does not cure this statutory defect.
• However, even though the landlord forfeited the right to keep the deposit, Florida law does not prevent them from separately pursuing damages in small claims court or through collections.

✅ Conclusion: The May 30 notice was untimely, and under Florida law Kairos cannot retain any portion of your deposit. Nonetheless, they may still attempt to assert a separate damages claim.

3. Proof of Damages
• Kairos relies primarily on a Home Depot RenoWalk estimate rather than receipts or proof of completed repairs.
• Florida law requires competent evidence of actual damages; estimates alone are insufficient.
• Broad repainting and full carpet replacement may constitute ordinary turnover or maintenance, not chargeable tenant damage. Hurricane-related issues are likewise not chargeable to tenants.

4. Collection Risk
• Kairos has applied your deposit and is asserting a balance of $7,418.47.
• While their claim is weak on both statutory (untimely notice) and evidentiary (no receipts) grounds, they may nevertheless continue to pursue the balance through debt collection efforts or litigation.

Recommendations
1. Demand Letter
– Send a formal written demand to Kairos citing Florida Statute §83.49, asserting that the May 30 notice was untimely and that Kairos has forfeited its right to retain the deposit.

2. Attach Proof
– Include documentation of:
• Your February 7 notice of intent to vacate.
• Kairos’s February 10 acknowledgment.
• April 15 vacate date (forwarding address email).
• May 30 claim notice.

3. Negotiated Resolution
– To align with your stated preference, you may offer a resolution in which Kairos retains the deposit but waives the balance of $7,418.47. This would resolve the matter consistent with your goal of avoiding continued collection activity or litigation.

4. Escalation if Necessary
– If negotiations fail, you may pursue a small claims action for return of the deposit. At the same time, if Kairos or its debt collectors continue to pursue the balance, you may dispute the debt under the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act and require validation.

Conclusion
Because you vacated on April 15, 2025, and Kairos did not issue its claim notice until May 30, 2025—45 days later—Kairos failed to comply with Florida Statute §83.49. The statute is triggered by the actual vacate date, not the lease end date. As a result, Kairos has forfeited its right to retain your deposit. However, they may still attempt to pursue separate damages. Given your preference that they simply retain the deposit and waive further charges, a negotiated resolution along those terms may be the most efficient path to avoid further collection efforts or litigation.

